## SUMMARY
#
Here’s how I understand the system and the decisions so far:

You’re building a “register” tool:
a small CLI that associates register names (strings) with register values stored in files.

Each register has:

a name (CLI-level handle),

a backing file path,

an exec flag (is this something runnable?),

a utf8 flag (treat as text vs opaque bytes).

Backing files can come from:

A blank file,

An existing file you copy from,

The X CLIPBOARD or PRIMARY selection via xclip.

Default behavior:

Registers live in ~/.register/ by default if --file not given (~/.register/<name>).

Default flags: exec = false, utf8 = true.

add should error if you try to reuse an existing register name.

Metadata:

Stored in ~/.register/register_metadata.json.

Top-level object mapping register names to { file, exec, utf8 }.

Pending design choices you’re now tightening:

Removal semantics (metadata only vs metadata+file, especially with shared files).

Separate add vs update instead of a single overwriting add, to keep behavior explicit and avoid accidental clobbering.




